  "summary": "Round 4 correctly identified stage-aware blocking, rejection-induced plan invalidation, the missing delete path, field defaulting, and concurrency as unresolved gaps. This round provides concrete, code-grounded resolutions for each: (1) Stage-aware blocking should be enforced only in _auto_run_check()'s impl_stages branch (approved, implementing, spec-writing, review, testing) — not in plan_stages (ideating, plan-inbox, reviewing-plan) — because planning and ideation can proceed in parallel with unfinished dependencies; the is_blocked() method itself should remain stage-agnostic (it reports dependency status), and the auto-run logic should decide when to respect it; (2) Rejection-triggered unblocking must flag downstream features for re-planning rather than silently unblocking — when move_to_stage() detects a terminal 'rejected' transition for a feature that appears in other features' depends_on lists, those downstream features should be moved back to plan-inbox with a history note like 'Dependency {title} was rejected — re-planning required', preventing stale plans from proceeding to implementation; (3) Since FeatureFile has no delete method and 'rejected' is just a stage directory, dangling reference cleanup should trigger inside move_to_stage() when the target stage is 'rejected' — scan same-board features and remove the rejected feature's ID from their depends_on lists, then save affected features; this is the only realistic cleanup point given the current architecture; (4) The depends_on field must default gracefully via self._data.get('depends_on', []) since hundreds of existing feature JSON files lack it — the FeatureFile constructor should normalize this on load, and save() should always persist it (even as empty list) to converge existing files to the new schema on next write; (5) The concurrency concern about move_to_stage() is real but low-priority for v1 — the pipeline server's _auto_run_check() is the primary mover of stages, it runs sequentially in a single event loop executor, and TUI/WebUI moves are human-initiated (inherently serialized by user interaction speed); adding file-level locking to move_to_stage() would complicate the codebase for a race condition that requires near-simultaneous human actions on the same feature. For the WebUI integration, api.go needs a new edit endpoint /api/clients/{id}/features/{fid}/edit-depends-on following the existing pattern of _cmd_edit_* handlers, and the FeatureSummary struct should include DependsOn []string and Blocked bool fields. The implementation order should be: data model changes in state.py first (field + defaulting + is_blocked() + cycle detection), then pipeline skip logic, then stage-transition hooks, then UI exposure — with each layer independently testable."
